  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Resource Development — Establish Accounts", "technique": "T1585.002", "what_happened": "At 2026-02-26 01:14:02 UTC, the external host 203.0.113.66 issued outbound DNS NS lookups for three freshly registered lookalike domains — corp-billing.io, trustedvendor-billing.com, corp-finance-update.net — each less than 14 days old (CTI freshness signal: domain_age=9 days for corp-billing.io). Suricata logged the DNS resolution traffic. This is the attacker confirming their established email infrastructure is delegated and live before sending. The lookalike domains impersonate a legitimate billing vendor.", "chain_reason": "Suricata DNS events with data.srcip=203.0.113.66 and data.dns.rrname matching the three lookalike domains, immediately preceding the SMTP injection burst from the same data.srcip. The narrow domain set + sub-14-day registration age is the Establish-Accounts:Email-Accounts fingerprint.", "first_seen": "2026-02-26T01:14:02+0000"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Initial Access / Phishing — BEC Delivery", "technique": "T1585.002", "what_happened": "At 01:18:41 UTC, 203.0.113.66 opened SMTP sessions to the corporate MTA 10.108.57.10 on port 25 and injected three messages: From accounts@corp-billing.io to finance.team@corp.local, From billing@trustedvendor-billing.com to hr.manager@corp.local, From ap@corp-finance-update.net to cfo@corp.local. All three carried the subject 'Updated Banking Details — Action Required' with a body requesting a payment-detail change for invoice #INV-2026-Q3-4412 to a new IBAN. The lookalike domains had SPF/DKIM configured so the messages passed authentication and were accepted by the gateway. Suricata logged the external SMTP connection; the Wazuh MTA syslog decoder fired a custom rule on domain_age<14 to a Finance recipient.", "chain_reason": "Suricata SMTP events with data.srcip=203.0.113.66, data.dstip=10.108.57.10, data.dstport=25 plus Wazuh rule.description matching the fresh-sender-domain-to-Finance custom rule, all within four minutes of the Step-1 DNS checks from the same source. From-domains exactly match the Step-1 lookalike set.", "first_seen": "2026-02-26T01:18:41+0000"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Initial Access / Phishing — Credential Harvesting Wave", "technique": "T1585.002", "what_happened": "At ~01:38 UTC, after a deliberate ~20-minute pause, 203.0.113.66 reconnected to MTA 10.108.57.10 and sent a second wave from the same three lookalike senders to the same Finance/HR/CFO recipients, this time with subject 'Verify your credentials — Corp Portal Update' and a body containing a credential-harvesting link hxxps://corp-portal-verify.corp-billing.io/login hosted on a subdomain of the attacker's own lookalike domain. The two-wave structure — BEC payment-fraud pretext first, credential-harvesting pretext second — is the Establish-Accounts follow-on playbook: monetise via fraud, then expand access.", "chain_reason": "Second cluster of Suricata SMTP events with data.srcip=203.0.113.66, data.dstip=10.108.57.10, data.dstport=25 plus Wazuh BEC-pattern custom rule (subject contains payment|invoice|banking|verify|credentials), ~20 minutes after the Step-2 burst, identical sender/recipient pairing. The credential-harvesting URL reuses the corp-billing.io lookalike domain established in Step 1.", "first_seen": "2026-02-26T01:38:05+0000"}
  ],
